WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:03.330
Hi guys, I've completed ten&nbsp;
weeks of Jump Science 2.0,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:06.690
and I'm here to review it and share&nbsp;
my results. If you weren't aware,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:11.610
I started last year with a 22-and-a-half-inch&nbsp;
vert that went up to 26 inches after Vert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:18.120
Shock. Then I spent nine or ten months doing&nbsp;
other things and my vert went back down to 23. This summer, 2019,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:24.120
I did the Vertical Jump Bible intermediate program&nbsp;
and got it back up to 26 and three-quarter inches.

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:27.060
That means, at 5'9" with a 7'5" reach,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:31.770
I am four and a quarter inches from the&nbsp;
rim and about thirteen inches from dunking.

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:36.480
Okay, Jump Science. This was suggested&nbsp;
to me by someone on Instagram (thank you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:40.200
Nikolai). It cost me thirty-seven&nbsp;
dollars, which is thirty-one pounds fifty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:44.730
and for that, you get access to six&nbsp;
different programs, Levels 1 to 6.

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:49.410
Level 1 is for people who can't do&nbsp;
a full range-of-motion goblet squat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:53.460
with a twenty-five kilo dumbbell. This is&nbsp;
just about establishing basic strength and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:57.990
movement proficiency. You're stuck at Level&nbsp;
2 if you can't squat one-and-a-half times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.990 --> 00:01:02.310
your body weight to a suitable depth. You're&nbsp;
then sorted by whether or not you can squat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.310 --> 00:01:06.600
twice your body weight and your relative vert,&nbsp;
which is your max vert divided by your height.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:09.390
He said to be honest with&nbsp;
yourself, and at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:12.510
I couldn't do a twice-bodyweight&nbsp;
squat, so I ended up on Level 3.

00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:17.520
That meant doing a 10-week program, which&nbsp;
was split into three three-week phases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:21.720
with a rest week in Week 7. Having&nbsp;
emailed the program's creator about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:25.140
the best retesting protocol, he&nbsp;
emphasised not giving too much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:29.730
weight to your max vert on any one day and&nbsp;
instead to look for long-term improvements.

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:34.140
As I needed to have something to show for&nbsp;
this program, I chose to retest 10 days after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:38.850
finishing it. Despite about 20 attempts,&nbsp;
I could only manage a best running vert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:44.880
of around 66 centimetres, which is less than&nbsp;
an inch lower than at the end of Jump Bible.

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:47.190
This felt about as fresh as I was going to be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:51.870
so it was quite frustrating to not jump&nbsp;
any higher. It's unfair to use my absolute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:56.340
best jump from after the last program as a&nbsp;
starting point for this one, but in Week 1,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.340 --> 00:02:01.320
I was jumping about the same height. So, based on&nbsp;
one testing day, I haven't gained much on my vert.

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:06.060
As with other programs, I also tested my&nbsp;
standing vert each week just to see where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:13.230
I'm at. Vert Shock got me 3.2 inches overall, but&nbsp;
2.4 in the first three weeks. Jump Bible was 2.4&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:18.840
and steady increase. Jump Science had a head&nbsp;
start, and despite dropping in Weeks 8 and 9,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:22.860
I finished up at 58 centimetres,&nbsp;
again roughly where I started.

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:26.910
I think it's important to measure both&nbsp;
because one is more about elastic strength,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.910 --> 00:02:29.130
while the other one is more raw power.

00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:33.030
Now, because it's a paid program,&nbsp;
I can't share the exact sessions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:35.520
but every week there are three workouts: A, B,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:41.490
and C. C starts the same every week. There's a&nbsp;
lot of practice jumping involved, and it's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:46.500
first program I've seen that specifically sets&nbsp;
aside time to practise the skill of jumping.

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:50.970
There's a series of members-only videos to&nbsp;
show you all the exercises and what to focus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:55.470
on. With the practice jumps, I found those&nbsp;
helpful. As someone who doesn't have much of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.470 --> 00:02:59.670
a background in jumping, it was nice to go&nbsp;
through the basic mechanics and understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.670 --> 00:03:04.500
the fundamentals of what I'm trying to achieve.
Rather than getting straight into depth jumps,

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:09.750
weeks 1, 2, and 3 have depth drops, also known as&nbsp;
shock jumps, which involve sticking the landing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:15.660
without actually jumping. This is to let your body&nbsp;
adapt to the high forces involved. There are also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.660 --> 00:03:22.080
single and two-legged jumps, with volume either&nbsp;
staying the same or going up by one set each week.

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:26.130
Weights had overhead squats,&nbsp;
mainly for increasing flexibility,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:31.020
then box squats and deadlifts, where&nbsp;
I subbed in hex bar deadlifts. These&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:34.860
were the usual progression of increasing&nbsp;
the weight and reducing the reps each week.

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:39.390
Weeks 4 to 6 had the same weights.&nbsp;
Depth jumps made an appearance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.390 --> 00:03:44.370
as did tuck jumps and broad jumps. I keep&nbsp;
mentioning depth jumps because they seem to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:48.270
be the backbone of every jump program.&nbsp;
The main point is being able to absorb&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.270 --> 00:03:52.620
the force and turn it into a jump at least&nbsp;
as high as you can achieve from standing.

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:55.140
For these, I worked off a rule I picked up online:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.140 --> 00:04:00.330
to be on the ground no longer than 0.3&nbsp;
seconds. You can check this with a video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.330 --> 00:04:04.080
of yourself by going frame by frame to&nbsp;
work out how long you're on the ground.

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:09.090
Week 7 was a rest, but he also had&nbsp;
a 10 to 15-minute lower-body stretching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:11.430
routine to be carried out daily across the whole&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:15.240
ten weeks. Little details like that&nbsp;
gave me confidence in the program.

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:19.770
Earlier this year, I did a hamstring&nbsp;
flexibility program, and I noticed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.770 --> 00:04:25.890
as a side effect of that, my standing vert went&nbsp;
up by about an inch. I felt less restricted and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:29.730
more fluid when I was doing my jumps,&nbsp;
so I know that stretching is important,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:32.280
and that's something he really&nbsp;
emphasises in this program.

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:37.920
In Weeks 8 to 10, you drop all the weights&nbsp;
and start to include exercises like sprinting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:42.420
and bounding. This felt a lot like how&nbsp;
Vert Shock is set up, and I was hoping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:46.680
it would give me a similar boost to my opening&nbsp;
weeks of that. The opposite was actually true.

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:51.060
If you look at my standing vert, it was&nbsp;
much weaker in Weeks 8 and 9. On the court,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:55.710
I was jumping about 64 centimetres,&nbsp;
about an inch and a half below my PB.

00:04:55.710 --> 00:05:00.240
Overall, in terms of the program design, my&nbsp;
first impressions were that it was well put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:05.220
together. He's catering to all levels, and&nbsp;
using things like strength and current vert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:09.180
relative to weight and height seemed like a&nbsp;
good way of determining someone's ability.

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:14.310
I was concerned in Week 8 when my standing&nbsp;
vert went down because, after a rest week,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.310 --> 00:05:19.140
I was expecting to jump maybe 24 inches&nbsp;
or higher, and I was feeling really fresh.

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:22.290
I slated Vert Shock at the time,&nbsp;
but having tried other programs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:25.770
those first few weeks on it have been&nbsp;
the biggest gains I've seen so far.

00:05:25.770 --> 00:05:31.710
Despite having only one day's testing to base it&nbsp;
on, I would not go around recommending Level 3&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.710 --> 00:05:35.730
to others. The practice jumps were great, but&nbsp;
that's something you could add to any program.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.730 --> 00:05:40.560
If I were to do this again, I would replace Weeks&nbsp;
8 to 10 with the first three weeks of Vert Shock.

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:43.110
Thanks for watching. If you have any questions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:46.740
then drop them in the comments section below,&nbsp;
and I'll try to get back to you. Cheers.

